      </div>

      <p>Iranian state media on September 5-8 maintained two simultaneous editorial tracks without publishing a reconciliation between them. IRNA's English service covered the September 5 ballistic missile launch as a "legitimate defensive response to ongoing American aggression against Iranian vessels," quoting IRGC Aerospace Force Commander Hajizadeh (IRNA, September 5). The same IRNA feed carried no reference to the Baghaei corridor-deal statement published in Bloomberg on September 7, maintaining domestic editorial separation between the IRGC military narrative and the MFA diplomatic track. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>FM Araghchi's August 19 statement — "Iran rejects ceasefire and demands complete end to the war" — remained the last unconditional MFA position on file as of September 8 (IRNA, August 19). President Pezeshkian's September 1 SCO speech offered a conditional reciprocation formula; Kayhan newspaper, the hardline daily with editorial proximity to the Supreme Leader's office, published an editorial on September 6 characterizing the June MOU's nuclear provisions as a "trap designed to permanently cap Iran's capabilities" (Kayhan, September 6). The Araghchi-Pezeshkian divide on conditionality went unreported in IRNA English, while Kayhan's characterization of the MOU received no rebuttal from MFA-affiliated outlets in the same 48-hour window. → supports <span class="cite">F3</span> without move</p>

      <p>SNSC Secretary Rezaei's September 7 "within days" statement, as carried by Tasnim News and IranWarLive, appeared absent from IRNA's English feed as of September 8, creating an attribution gap between the military-track message and the English-language diplomatic feed. Press TV ran a brief summary without attributing the statement to Rezaei by name. The absence of IRNA English coverage on the exclusion zone statement alongside Kayhan's September 6 MOU editorial — both published in the same news cycle with no MFA rebuttal in English-language state outlets — is consistent with tactical editorial silence before a formal announcement. → moved <span class="cite">F2</span></p>

    </div>
